# Godot 4 version control rules for where-is-school.
#
# Godot 4 splits "metadata that must be committed" from "cache that must not".
# Getting this wrong is the usual reason an editor session dirties the tree with
# dozens of files, so the two are kept in separate sections below.
#
# Rule of thumb: commit anything the engine cannot regenerate (source files,
# assets, .import sidecars, .uid sidecars); ignore anything it regenerates from
# those (everything under .godot/).
#
# A NOTE ON NEGATION PATTERNS
# ---------------------------
# This file deliberately contains NO `!` negations. Two reasons, both of which
# this project's rule set has already run into:
#
#   1. Git evaluates every pattern in order and the LAST match wins. A blanket
#      `!*.gd` or `!*.png` near the end of the file therefore also matches paths
#      *inside* an ignored directory and re-includes the cache that the
#      `.godot/` rule exists to exclude. Order alone cannot express "ignore this
#      directory except these extensions" safely when the exception list is
#      broad -- the negations have to be enumerated per extension, and every new
#      asset type added later re-opens the hole.
#   2. A negation cannot resurrect a file whose parent directory is excluded, so
#      a broad negation block gives a false sense of safety anyway.
#
# The never-ignore list lives at the bottom of this file as an explicit,
# human-readable inventory instead. Correctness comes from *not* writing a rule
# that could catch source in the first place: notice that this file only ever
# ignores caches, junk and build output, and never a Godot source or asset
# extension. That is what keeps `*.import`, `*.uid`, `*.glb`, `*.wav`, `*.ttf`
# and `project.godot` tracked -- not a negation, just the absence of a rule.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Godot import and editor cache -- regenerated on first open, never commit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Godot 4's single cache directory: imported resources, the UID cache, editor
# layout state, shader cache, script doc cache, filesystem scan cache. Roughly
# 40 MB on this project, and it changes every time the editor opens it.
.godot/

# Godot 3's equivalent. Not used by a 4.x project, but a stale directory left
# behind by an older toolchain must never be committed by accident.
.import/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Compiled and generated build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Binary translation files produced by the export pipeline from the JSON
# translation sources in data/locale/. Regenerated per export.
*.translation

# Export configuration may contain local paths or credentials.
export.cfg
export_presets.cfg

# Local exports and build outputs.
.export/
builds/
dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Godot crash output written inside the project folder
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Note: a plain `*.log` rule is deliberately NOT used. The two capture logs in
# docs/screenshots/ are committed CI evidence (see docs/TECHNICAL_ROADMAP.md),
# and a blanket `*.log` would silently untrack them. Only the engine's own crash
# and rotation artefacts are ignored, and `godot.log` is named exactly rather
# than by pattern.
godot.log
*.log.old
*.crash

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Operating system and editor junk
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

*.tmp
*.bak
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
.Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Local toolchain and personal environment
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

.vscode/
.idea/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/

# Blender autosave and crash files. The .blend sources themselves are committed;
# these are throwaway recovery files written next to them.
*.blend1
*.blend2
*.blend@

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. NEVER IGNORE -- the list this file is reviewed against
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing below needs a rule of its own: no pattern above matches these
# extensions or names, so Git tracks them by default. The list is recorded here
# so a future edit can be checked against it, and so the two common mistakes are
# named explicitly.
#
# Resource metadata a clone cannot be reconstructed without:
#   *.import   Godot 4 per-asset import sidecar, e.g.
#              assets/props/playground_bench/bench_playground.glb.import
#              This is NOT the same thing as the Godot 3 `.import/` DIRECTORY
#              ignored in section 1. Without the sidecar, Godot re-imports each
#              asset with default settings on a fresh clone and the committed
#              import options are lost. Do not add `*.import` as a rule.
#   *.uid      Godot 4 resource UID sidecar, e.g. scripts/ui/hud.gd.uid.
#              Pairs a path with a stable uid:// so that renaming or moving a
#              file does not break references that cite the UID. Do not add
#              `*.uid` as a rule.
#
# Project and source:
#   project.godot, *.gd, *.gdshader, *.gdshaderinc, *.tscn, *.tres, *.res
#
# 3D, audio, font and image assets the game uses:
#   *.glb, *.gltf, *.blend, *.fbx, *.obj, *.wav, *.ogg, *.mp3, *.flac,
#   *.ttf, *.otf, *.png, *.jpg, *.jpeg, *.webp, *.svg, *.exr, *.hdr
#
# Authored data, tooling and documentation:
#   *.json, *.csv, *.md, *.txt, *.yml, *.yaml, *.py
#
# Committed evidence (see section 3):
#   docs/screenshots/PLAYTHROUGH.log, docs/screenshots/UI_CAPTURE.log
